--- memo.md ---
# Adjudication: matching the NSW treated to CPS controls

**Bottom line: recovery is only under favorable specifications, not robustly.** The randomized NSW contrast is an estimate of the program's ATT among its 185 treated participants: $1,794. Replacing the randomized controls with CPS controls produces a raw contrast of $-8,498, a gap of $-10,292. That failure is unsurprising. CPS members and NSW trainees enter the comparison with sharply different observed profiles and may differ in unmeasured earnings prospects. The raw CPS comparison is not a credible counterfactual.

Conditioning on the listed covariates changes the comparison to CPS people with similar *observed* age, schooling, race/ethnicity, marital status, degree status, and, in the richer model, prior earnings. The score is a device for balancing those measured variables; matching or stratifying on it does not randomize CPS membership, repair mismeasured histories, or remove selection on unobserved employability, motivation, local labor markets, and program referral. Nor does agreement validate omitted functional forms or extrapolation from rare high-score CPS cases. The relevant identifying claim remains conditional exchangeability plus overlap, neither of which is created by a logit model.

The eight adjusted estimates span $-4,046 to $2,088. The closest is the 1-nn matching specification with demographics + prior earnings and common-support trimming = No: $1,712 (gap $-82). Only 3 of 8 adjusted 95% intervals cover the experimental point estimate. That is not the stability one would expect if the CPS adjustment were validating a generally reliable recovery procedure. In this data, trimming retains every NSW treated unit because the CPS score range covers them; it only removes out-of-range CPS controls. It can therefore change a stratified control mean, but it does not turn the CPS comparison into an experiment.

The uncertainty display is intentionally conservative about its scope. For fixed-one-nearest-neighbour matching with replacement, it uses an Abadie--Imbens-style analytic variance with control-reuse counts rather than an ordinary bootstrap, which is not valid for this nonsmooth estimator. The stratified rows use fixed-strata sampling variances. Neither interval includes uncertainty from choosing and fitting the propensity model, so visual agreement with the benchmark is a diagnostic, not a formal proof of identification.

A paper may claim that some specified propensity-score adjustments move the CPS comparison markedly toward the randomized NSW benchmark, and may report the particular specifications that do so. It may not claim, without qualification, that propensity-score matching recovers the experimental effect, that balancing observables establishes ignorability, or that one favorable estimate validates the procedure for another comparison sample. The appropriate conclusion is specification-dependent corroboration: the Dehejia--Wahba-style success is possible, while the Smith--Todd warning about sensitivity to covariates and analysis choices is the better description of the full curve.


